public with sharing class CasePriorityHandler {

    public static void handleBeforeInsert(List<Case> newCases) {
        if (newCases == null || newCases.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        Set<Id> accountIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (Case c : newCases) {
            if (c.AccountId != null) {
                accountIds.add(c.AccountId);
            }
        }

        if (accountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        Map<Id, Account> accountsById = new Map<Id, Account>(
            [SELECT Id, Rating FROM Account WHERE Id IN :accountIds]
        );

        for (Case c : newCases) {
            if (c.AccountId == null) {
                continue;
            }
            Account acc = accountsById.get(c.AccountId);
            if (acc != null && acc.Rating == 'Hot') {
                c.Priority = 'High';
            }
        }
    }
}
```

**Deviation:** I am not making a synchronous callout from the trigger, and not one request per account. Salesforce does not support callouts directly from a trigger (they must run asynchronously), and a synchronous trigger callout would also hold the transaction open. One-per-account would exceed the limit of 100 callouts per transaction for the 200-record marketplace batches. The ERP API explicitly accepts an array of 1–200 customers, so I batch all of `Trigger.new` into a single `Queueable` callout (chunked at 200), which is bulk-safe, traceable by `salesforceId`, and preferred over `@future` for typed, serializable parameters and job monitoring.

