/**
 * Creates follow-up Tasks for Opportunities coming out of the weekly pipeline review.
 *
 * DEVIATION FROM THE REQUESTED "STRAIGHTFORWARD" IMPLEMENTATION, AND WHY:
 * The requested approach (one `insert` per Task inside a try/catch) cannot work
 * for the stated volumes. A transaction may issue at most 150 DML statements,
 * and the review passes 150-250 Opportunities in a single call, so the loop
 * would hit the DML statements governor limit part-way through the list.
 * A LimitException cannot be caught by a try/catch, so the whole transaction
 * (including Tasks already inserted) would roll back and no Tasks would persist.
 *
 * This implementation achieves exactly the same business semantics — one Task
 * failing validation must not block the others, and the caller learns which
 * Opportunities failed — with bulk-safe operations:
 *   - 1 SOQL query for all requested Opportunities (no SOQL in a loop).
 *   - 1 DML statement using Database.insert(tasks, false) (allOrNone = false),
 *     which gives partial success: valid Tasks commit, Tasks rejected by
 *     validation rules (or any other per-record error) return an unsuccessful
 *     Database.SaveResult instead of throwing, and only those Opportunity Ids
 *     are reported back to the caller.
 */
public with sharing class FollowUpTaskService {

    /**
     * Creates one follow-up Task per Opportunity.
     *
     * @param opportunityIds Ids of the Opportunities to follow up on.
     * @return Ids of the Opportunities whose Task could not be created
     *         (empty when every Task was created). Opportunities that do
     *         not exist are also reported as failed, as no Task can be
     *         created for them.
     */
    public static Set<Id> createFollowUps(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        Set<Id> failedOpportunityIds = new Set<Id>();

        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOpportunityIds;
        }

        // De-duplicate (and drop nulls) so each Opportunity gets at most one Task.
        Set<Id> uniqueOpportunityIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (Id opportunityId : opportunityIds) {
            if (opportunityId != null) {
                uniqueOpportunityIds.add(opportunityId);
            }
        }
        if (uniqueOpportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOpportunityIds;
        }

        // Single bulk query.
        Map<Id, Opportunity> opportunitiesById = new Map<Id, Opportunity>([
            SELECT Id, Name, OwnerId
            FROM Opportunity
            WHERE Id IN :uniqueOpportunityIds
        ]);

        // Any requested Opportunity that cannot be found cannot get a Task.
        for (Id opportunityId : uniqueOpportunityIds) {
            if (!opportunitiesById.containsKey(opportunityId)) {
                failedOpportunityIds.add(opportunityId);
            }
        }

        Date dueDate = Date.today().addDays(7);

        List<Task> tasksToInsert = new List<Task>();
        // Parallel list: the Opportunity Id each Task (by index) belongs to,
        // so per-record results can be mapped back to Opportunities.
        List<Id> taskOpportunityIds = new List<Id>();

        for (Opportunity opp : opportunitiesById.values()) {
            tasksToInsert.add(new Task(
                WhatId       = opp.Id,
                OwnerId      = opp.OwnerId,
                Subject      = 'Follow up: ' + opp.Name,
                ActivityDate = dueDate,
                Status       = 'Not Started',
                Priority     = 'Normal'
            ));
            taskOpportunityIds.add(opp.Id);
        }

        if (tasksToInsert.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOpportunityIds;
        }

        // Single bulk DML with partial success: a Task rejected by a
        // validation rule fails alone and does not roll back the others,
        // and no exception is thrown for per-record failures.
        List<Database.SaveResult> results = Database.insert(tasksToInsert, false);

        for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
            if (!results[i].isSuccess()) {
                failedOpportunityIds.add(taskOpportunityIds[i]);
            }
        }

        return failedOpportunityIds;
    }
}
```

**What I did differently, and why**

1.  **One `@future` call per transaction, not one per Opportunity.** A transaction may make at most 50 `@future` calls. Calling `recalculate(opp.AccountId)` inside the loop would throw `System.LimitException: Too many future calls: 51` part-way through a 200-record CPQ batch and roll the whole save back. Instead the trigger collects the affected accounts into a deduplicated `Set<Id>` and makes a single `@future` call — `Set<Id>` is a legal `@future` parameter, and dedup also avoids recalculating the same account repeatedly. This changes the signature to `recalculate(Set<Id>)`.
2.  **Also recalculated on `AccountId` change, delete and undelete.** Otherwise moving an Opportunity would leave the *old* account stale forever, and deleting the last open Opportunity would never demote an account to `Cold`. On reparenting, both the old and new accounts are recalculated. On update the field-change filter (`StageName`/`Amount`/`AccountId`) is preserved, so unrelated edits stay cheap.
3.  **Null `AccountId` is skipped, and a synchronous fallback is used if already in an async context.** `@future` cannot be invoked from a future/batch context (it would throw); if the CPQ integration ever runs inside one, the same logic runs inline rather than failing the save. Accounts are only written when `Rating` actually changes, minimising DML, workflow/trigger churn and lock contention.

