/**
 * Matches leads to existing contacts by email address.
 *
 * Why not a nested loop:
 * With up to 3,000 leads and 3,000 contacts, a nested loop performs up to
 * 9,000,000 comparisons in the worst case (when most leads do not match),
 * repeatedly normalizing the same contact emails on every comparison. Inside
 * a synchronous import transaction this risks the Apex CPU time limit.
 *
 * Instead this builds a Map keyed by normalized contact email once, then
 * resolves each lead with a single map lookup: O(leads + contacts) work,
 * each email normalized exactly once, with identical semantics — including
 * "first matching contact in list order wins", because a map entry is never
 * overwritten once created.
 *
 * This class performs no SOQL and no DML; it works purely on the records
 * passed in memory.
 */
public with sharing class LeadContactMatcher {

    /**
     * Returns a list the same size and order as {@code leads}: for each lead,
     * the Id of the first contact in {@code contacts} whose email matches the
     * lead's email ignoring case and leading/trailing whitespace, or null if
     * there is no match. Leads or contacts with a blank email never match.
     *
     * @param leads    the leads to match (may be null or empty)
     * @param contacts the candidate contacts, in priority order (may be null or empty)
     * @return contact Ids (or nulls) aligned by index with {@code leads}
     */
    public static List<Id> matchByEmail(List<Lead> leads, List<Contact> contacts) {
        List<Id> results = new List<Id>();
        if (leads == null) {
            return results;
        }

        // Index contacts by normalized email; first contact per email wins.
        Map<String, Id> firstContactIdByEmail = new Map<String, Id>();
        if (contacts != null) {
            for (Contact contactRecord : contacts) {
                if (contactRecord == null || String.isBlank(contactRecord.Email)) {
                    continue;
                }
                String normalizedEmail = normalize(contactRecord.Email);
                if (!firstContactIdByEmail.containsKey(normalizedEmail)) {
                    firstContactIdByEmail.put(normalizedEmail, contactRecord.Id);
                }
            }
        }

        for (Lead leadRecord : leads) {
            if (leadRecord == null || String.isBlank(leadRecord.Email)) {
                results.add(null);
            } else {
                results.add(firstContactIdByEmail.get(normalize(leadRecord.Email)));
            }
        }
        return results;
    }

    private static String normalize(String email) {
        return email.trim().toLowerCase();
    }
}
```

**What I changed and why**

The requested one-job-per-case design was not implemented, because it breaks in bulk:

* `System.enqueueJob` allows at most 50 jobs per synchronous transaction (1 from an asynchronous context). Closing 200 cases would throw `System.LimitException: Too many queueable jobs added to the queue` on the 51st job, rolling back the entire `Case` update — agents could not close cases at all.
* Changed to: the trigger collects all newly-closed `Id`s and enqueues **one** `CaseSurveyJob` carrying a `Set<Id>`. This handles 200+ records in a single transaction and a single job.
* Per-record isolation (the intent behind one-job-per-case) is preserved inside the job by `Database.insert(tasks, false)`, so one bad `Task` cannot fail the others.
* The job accepts only `Id`s (small, serializable Queueable state), re-queries for `CaseNumber`/`ContactId`, and re-checks `IsClosed` and `ContactId != null` at execution time, so a case reopened before the job runs gets no survey task. Tasks are built and inserted in bulk (one SOQL, one DML).

